From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Subject: [PATCH] Capture monitors on the Apple silicon display controller

On Apple silicon Macs running Linux the display controller (DRM driver
"apple") is a KMS-only device: the panel and the external outputs hang
off it, while rendering happens on the separate GPU device, which has the
render node and no connectors. Like evdi it has no render node of its
own, so it was skipped as a monitor card, no monitor was ever found and
monitor capture failed with "no usable output found".

Treat it the way evdi is treated: a card with connectors is a monitor
card even without a render node. OpenGL and the video encoder then run on
the GPU (the monitor card has no render node) and KMS capture uses the
display controller through kms_card_path, as for DisplayLink monitors.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: LLM
---
--- a/src/utils.c
+++ b/src/utils.c
@@ -538,7 +538,12 @@ static bool drm_fd_is_hardware_accelerated_gpu(int drm_fd) {
         return false;
 
     bool hardware_accelerated = false;
-    if(strcmp(ver->name, "evdi") == 0) {
+    /*
+     * KMS-only display devices (DisplayLink evdi, the Apple silicon display controller) have no render node: their
+     * framebuffers are rendered on another gpu. They count as monitor cards when they have connectors; opengl and the
+     * video encoder then run on the rendering gpu and the kms capture uses the display device (kms_card_path).
+     */
+    if(strcmp(ver->name, "evdi") == 0 || strcmp(ver->name, "apple") == 0) {
         drmModeResPtr resources = drmModeGetResources(drm_fd);
         hardware_accelerated = resources != NULL && resources->count_connectors > 0;
         if(resources)
